#
# timestamp-multi-line.formats
#
# Timestamp formats for multi-line-mode(timestamp), one strptime(3) format per
# line.  A line that starts with one of these timestamps starts a new event,
# any other line continues the previous one.  Lines starting with '#' are
# comments.
#
# These formats are only consulted when the built-in scanners fail, and
# strptime() is a lot slower than those, so the common formats are built in
# and recognized without any configuration:
#
#   2026-10-09T15:31:50.123456+02:00   ISO 8601 / RFC 3339, 'T' or space
#   2026-10-09 15:31:50,123            separated, optional fraction and zone
#   Oct  9 15:31:50                    BSD syslog (RFC 3164), optional fraction
#   Oct 09 2026 15:31:50:              Cisco
#   Oct 09 15:31:50 2026               Linksys
#   <134>Oct  9 15:31:50                a syslog PRI, and the "1 " version of
#   <13>1 2026-10-09T15:31:50Z          RFC 5424, are skipped before the timestamp
#   10-09-2026 15:31:50.456 +0000      Splunk's own logs, US and European
#   10/09/2026 15:31:50                numeric dates with '-', '/' or '.',
#   09.10.2026 15:31:50                year first or last, optional fraction
#   2026/10/09 15:31:50                and zone (nginx error log, glog)
#   09/Oct/2026:15:31:50 +0200         Apache httpd and nginx access log
#   Thu, 09 Oct 2026 15:31:50 +0200    RFC 2822, optional day of week
#   09-Oct-2026 15:31:50.123           Tomcat
#   Thu Oct 09 15:31:50.123456 2026    Apache httpd error log
#   Oct 09, 2026 3:31:50 PM            java.util.logging
#   09 October 2026 15:31:50           month names in full, in the two above
#
# A timestamp may be enclosed in one of the multi-line-timestamp-pairs()
# characters, "[]" by default: "[2026-10-09 15:31:50] message".
#
# Nothing is enabled below: everything common is built in.  The formats are
# tried in this order, so keep the specific ones before the
# ambiguous ones.
#

#
# The formats below are ambiguous enough to break an event on a line that
# merely starts with a number.  Enabling one applies to every multi-line-mode(timestamp) source.
#

# seconds since the epoch: "1791561960", "1791561960.123456"
#%s
#%s.%f

# "mashed" dates without separators: "20261009 153150", "20261009153150"
#%Y%m%d %H%M%S
#%Y%m%d%H%M%S
#%y%m%d %H%M%S

# time of day only: "15:31:50"; many application logs print no date at all
#%H:%M:%S
#%H:%M:%S.%f

# date only: "2026-10-09"
#%Y-%m-%d
